## Why This Is Asked
Beginners must design practical ETL workflows that handle mixed formats and small drift, using Glue Studio in repeatable ways.

## Key Concepts
- Glue Studio ETL
- JSON flattening and CSV parsing
- Parquet partitioning by date
- Glue Data Catalog table
- Basic data quality checks and schema drift

## Code Example
```javascript
function flatten(obj, prefix = '', res = {}) {
  for (const k in obj) {
    const v = obj[k];
    const key = prefix ? prefix + '.' + k : k;
    if (v && typeof v === 'object' && !Array.isArray(v)) flatten(v, key, res);
    else res[key] = v;
  }
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test handling of occasionally missing fields?
- What would you do to scale the job for larger batches?

## Why This Is Asked
Practical beginner task validating end-to-end ingestion, data quality, and cataloging.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event-driven ETL
- Glue Spark/DynamicFrame validation
- Quarantine routing for bad records
- Parquet partitioning by date
- Glue Data Catalog and Athena access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo Glue-like JS snippet for validation
function process(batch){
  const valid=[], quarantined=[];
  for (const r of batch){
    if (r.a != null && r.b != null) valid.push(transform(r));
    else quarantined.push({row:r, validation_reason:'missing_required_fields'});
  }
  writeParquet(valid, datePartition);
  writeJson(quarantined, quarantineBucket);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema evolution if new fields are added?
- How would you monitor the quarantine rate and alert if it spikes?
